```

[PLUGIN_ROOT/src/StorefrontSubscriber.php]

```php [PLUGIN_ROOT/src/StorefrontSubscriber.php]
class StorefrontSubscriber implements EventSubscriberInterface {
    public static function getSubscribedEvents(): array
    {
        return [
            FooterPageletLoadedEvent::class => 'addActiveProductCount'
        ];
    }

    public function addActiveProductCount(FooterPageletLoadedEvent $event): void
    {
        if ($event->getRequest()->attributes->get('_route') !== 'frontend.footer') {
            return;
        }

        // Get the data from event and set it on headerParameters.
        $headerParameters = $event->getParameter('headerParameters') ?? [];
        $headerParameters['vendorPrefixPluginName']['activeProductCount'] = $event->getData('activeProductCount');

        $event->setParameter('headerParameters', $headerParameters);
    }
}
```

[PLUGIN_ROOT/src/StorefrontSubscriber.php]

```php [PLUGIN_ROOT/src/StorefrontSubscriber.php]
class StorefrontSubscriber implements EventSubscriberInterface {
    public static function getSubscribedEvents(): array
    {
        return [
            FooterPageletLoadedEvent::class => 'addActiveProductCount'
        ];
    }

    public function addActiveProductCount(FooterPageletLoadedEvent $event): void
    {
        if ($event->getRequest()->attributes->get('_route') !== 'frontend.footer') {
            return;
        }

        // Get the data from event and set it on headerParameters.
        $headerParameters = $event->getParameter('headerParameters') ?? [];
        $headerParameters['vendorPrefixPluginName']['activeProductCount'] = $event->getData('activeProductCount');

        $event->setParameter('headerParameters', $headerParameters);
    }
}
```

In the above code, we are subscribing to the `FooterPageletLoadedEvent` and adding a custom data point (`activeProductCount`) that will be available in the footer template. The `addActiveProductCount` method is called when this event is fired, allowing us to fetch our data from the Store API or any other source and set it on the event's data object.

In the subscriber class, we are checking if the route matches 'frontend.footer' before setting the data in headerParameters. This ensures that the data is only added for the footer pagelet.

Finally, you need to register your subscriber as an event listener by tagging it with `kernel.event_subscriber` in your services configuration file.

To define a custom entity for your own database table with the Data Abstraction Layer (DAL), follow these steps based on the provided documentation:

1. Create or extend an `EntityDefinition` class in your plugin's directory, e.g., `<plugin root>/src/Core/Content/Example/ExampleDefinition.php`. This class extends `Shopware\Core\Framework\DataAbstractionLayer\EntityDefinition`, which requires you to implement two methods: `getEntityName` and `defineFields`.

2. In the `defineFields` method, return a `FieldCollection` instance containing your entity's fields. You can create custom field classes like `IdField`, `StringField`, or `BoolField` as needed. For example:

```php
protected function defineFields(): FieldCollection
{
    return new FieldCollection([
        (new IdField('id', 'id'))->addFlags(new Required(), new PrimaryKey()),
        (new StringField('name', 'name')),
        (new StringField('description', 'description')),
        (new BoolField('active', 'active'))
    ]);
}
```

3. Override the `getEntityClass` method in your `ExampleDefinition` class to specify the custom entity class, e.g., `ExampleEntity`. This is done by extending `Shopware\Core\Framework\DataAbstractionLayer\Entity`, and defining properties for each field defined in the `defineFields` method.

```php
protected ?string $name;
protected ?string $description;
protected bool $active;
```

4. Implement getters and setters for these properties to allow setting and getting their values.

5. Finally, set your custom entity class in the `getEntityClass` method of your `ExampleDefinition`:

```php
class ExampleDefinition extends EntityDefinition
{
    [...]

    public function getEntityClass(): string
    {
        return ExampleEntity::class;
    }
}
```

By following these steps, you define a custom entity for your database table using the Shopware 6 Data Abstraction Layer.

To fetch products by name using the product repository, you can use the `search` method with a `Criteria` object that includes an `EqualsFilter` for the `name` field. Here's how you can do it:

```php
public function readData(Context $context): void
{
    $criteria = new Criteria();
    $criteria->addFilter(new EqualsFilter('name', 'Example name'));

    $products = $this->productRepository->search($criteria, $context);
}
```
This example will search for all products with the name `Example name` and return an `EntitySearchResult` containing all matched products. Since the `EntitySearchResult` is extending the `EntityCollection`, which is iterable, you could iterate over the results using a `foreach`.

To filter the `productReviews` association so that you only get products with product-reviews having a rating of at least 4 stars, you can use the `getAssociation` method to access and modify the associated criteria object. Here's how you can do it:

```php
public function readData(Context $context): void {
    // This will return products that have at least one review with points >= 4.
    $criteria = new Criteria();
    $this->productRepository->search($criteria, $context);
}

// Inside the search method of your repository:
$criteria->getAssociation('productReviews')->addFilter(new RangeFilter('points', [RangeFilter::GTE => 4]));

$product = $this->productRepository->search($criteria, $context)->first();
```

This code will first create a `Criteria` object and then use the `getAssociation` method to access the `productReviews` association. It adds a filter that only includes reviews with points greater than or equal to 4.

Remember to adjust your repository's search method accordingly, as shown in the example above.

To create or update entities using the `upsert` method, you need to provide the entity name and a payload array. The payload should be an array of associative arrays where each associative array represents the payload for one entity. Here's how you can use this in your code:

```twig
{% do services.writer.upsert('product', [
    { 'id': 1, 'name': 'New name' }
]) %}
```
In this example, if a product with `id` of 1 already exists, it will be updated. If not, a new product entity will be created.

For more details and examples, refer to the [upsert() method documentation](https://developer.shopware.com/docs/resources/references/app-reference/script-reference/custom-endpoint-script-services-reference.html#upsert).

To compute the average review rating of products using a DAL aggregation, you can use an `Avg` aggregation. Here's how you can set it up in both PHP Criteria and API Criteria:

**PHP Criteria:"
```php
criteria = new Criteria();
criteria->setLimit(1);
criteria->addAggregation(new AvgAggregation('avg-rating', 'reviewRatingField'));
result = $repository->search($criteria, $context);
/** @var AvgResult $aggregation **
$aggregation = $result->getAggregations()->get('avg-rating');
$aggregation->getAvg();"

**API Criteria:"
```json
{
    "limit": 1,
    "includes": {
        "product": ["id", "name"]
    },
    "aggregations": [
 {   
            "name": "avg-rating",
            "type": "avg",
            "field": "reviewRatingField"
 }
]
}
```

To override a block of the storefront header logo template in your plugin, you need to extend from the original `logo.html.twig` file and replace or append content to the desired block. Here's how you can do it:

```twig [PLUGIN_ROOT/src/Resources/views/storefront/layout/header/logo.html.twig]
{% sw_extends '@Storefront/storefront/layout/header/logo.html.twig' %}

{% block layout_header_logo_link %}
    <h2>Hello world!</h2>
{% endblock %}
```

This code snippet extends from the original `logo.html.twig` file and overrides the `layout_header_logo_link` block by replacing its contents with a simple `<h2>` tag containing 'Hello world!'.

If you want to append your text to the logo instead of replacing it, you could add a line like this to your override: <code v-pre>{{ parent() }}</code>

This approach works both in plugins and apps. In plugins, you can also use the `StorefrontRenderEvent` to add custom data to the header and footer.

To register a storefront JavaScript plugin that only runs on elements with a specific data attribute, you can use the `PluginManager.register()` method and provide an optional CSS selector. Here's how you do it:

```javascript
// <plugin root>/src/Resources/app/storefront/src/main.js
const PluginManager = window.PluginManager;
PluginManager.register('ExamplePlugin', ExamplePlugin, '[data-example-plugin]');
```
In this example, the plugin `ExamplePlugin` will be executed only if the HTML document contains at least one element with the `data-example-plugin` attribute. You can then use `this.el` inside your plugin to access the DOM elements it is bound to.

To create your own Store API endpoint in a plugin, you need to follow these steps based on the provided documentation:

1. **Create a Route Class**: Define your route class extending `AbstractExampleRoute` and use the `#[Route]` attribute to specify the path, name, HTTP methods, and entity.
2. **Inject Repository**: In your route class constructor, inject an instance of the repository for the entity you want to fetch.
3. **Implement Route Method**: Implement a method like `load()` that uses the criteria and context to search entities using the injected repository and returns a response object.
4. **Register Routes**: Ensure routes are registered in your plugin's configuration file, typically `routes.php` or similar, by importing route definitions from core and storefront directories.
5. **Handle Requests**: Use services like `http-client` for making requests to your custom API endpoints from the Storefront.

Here is an example of a route class:

```php
// <plugin root>/src/Core/Content/Example/SalesChannel/ExampleRoute.php
<?php declare(strict_types=1)=>
namespace Swag\BasicExample\Core\Content\Example\SalesChannel;
use Shopware\Core\Framework\Routing\StoreApiRouteScope;
use Shopware\Core\PlatformRequest;
use Shopware\Core\System\SalesChannel\SalesChannelContext;

#[Route(defaults: [PlatformRequest::ATTRIBUTE_ROUTE_SCOPE => [StoreApiRouteScope::ID]])]
class ExampleRoute extends AbstractExampleRoute
{
    protected EntityRepository $exampleRepository;

    public function __construct(EntityRepository $exampleRepository)
    {
        $this->exampleRepository = $exampleRepository;
    }

    public function getDecorated(): AbstractExampleRoute
    {
        throw new DecorationPatternException(self::class);
    }

    #[Route(path: '/store-api/example', name: 'store-api.example.search', methods: ['GET','POST'], defaults: ['_entity' => 'swag_example'])]
    public function load(Criteria $criteria, SalesChannelContext $context): ExampleRouteResponse
    {
        return new ExampleRouteResponse($this->exampleRepository->search($criteria, $context->getContext()));
    }
}
```

For more details and examples, refer to the [Add Store API Route](https://developer.shopware.com/docs/guides/plugins/plugins/framework/store-api/add-store-api-route.html) guide.

To change the cart at runtime and add a custom discount line item, you need to use both a `CartDataCollector` and a `CartProcessor`. The collector is used to collect all new prices necessary for your line items. Here's how you can implement this in your plugin:

1. Implement the `Shopware\Core\Checkout\Cart\CartDataCollectorInterface` interface in your class, which requires implementing the `collectData` method.
2. In the `collectData` method of your collector class, create and add a new line item for the discount using the `LineItemFactoryRegistry`. You can use the `addDiscountLineItem` method to add a discount line item with an ID, label, and value.
3. Implement the `Shopware\Core\Checkout\Cart\CartProcessorInterface` interface in your class, which requires implementing the `process` method. In this method, you should fetch the new prices from the `CartDataCollector` and calculate the actual price of that line item using a suitable calculator like `QuantityPriceCalculator` or `PercentagePriceCalculator`, depending on whether it's an absolute or relative discount.
4. Register your collector and processor classes in your `services.php` file, ensuring they are registered with the correct priority to ensure they run after other processors.

Here is a simplified example of how you might implement these steps:

```php
use Shopware\Core\Checkout\Cart\DataCollector\CartDataCollectorInterface;
use Shopware\Core\Checkout\Cart\LineItemFactoryRegistry;
use Shopware\Core\Checkout\Cart\ProcessorInterface;
use Shopware\Core\Checkout\Cart\Price\QuantityPriceCalculator;

class CustomDiscountCollector implements CartDataCollectorInterface {
    public function collectData(CartDataCollector $data): void {
        // Create and add a discount line item using LineItemFactoryRegistry
        $lineItemId = LineItemFactoryRegistry::addDiscountLineItem(
            'my-custom-discount',
            'A custom 10% discount',
            new PercentagePriceDefinition(10, 'EUR'),
            QuantityPriceCalculator::calculateTotalPrice($data->getCart(), ['default' => 20.0]) // Adjust the total price as needed
        );
    }
}

use Shopware\Core\Checkout\Cart\ProcessorInterface;
use Shopware\Core\Checkout\Cart\Price\PercentagePriceCalculator;

class CustomDiscountProcessor implements ProcessorInterface {
    public function process(CartDataCollector $data): void {
        // Fetch the new prices from CartDataCollector and calculate the actual price of the discount line item
        $percentagePriceDefinition = PercentagePriceCalculator::calculateTotalPrice($data->getCart(), ['default' => 20.0]);
    }
}
```

Make sure to register these classes in your `services.php` file with a priority that ensures they run after other processors, e.g., `priority: 4500`. This will allow the discount to be applied correctly.

For more details and examples, refer to the [Change Price of Items in Cart](https://developer.shopware.com/docs/guides/plugins/plugins/checkout/cart/change-price-of-item) guide.
